IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `intl-nouns`

-- Antitrust Guidelines for International Enforcement and Cooperation (2017), section 4.1:
-- comity.
--
-- AGENCY ENFORCEMENT POLICY, NOT LAW. "In enforcing the federal antitrust laws, the Agencies
-- consider international comity" (4.1 [pdf page 30]). The factors are listed "Among other
-- things", and "The relative weight given to each factor depends on the facts and
-- circumstances of each case" [pdf page 31]: so the factors are reported, never weighed, and
-- no function here says whether comity counsels for or against enforcement. The definite
-- sentences are encoded apart: when no conflict of law exists (with n.144 on blocking
-- statutes), and that a foreign authority's action does not preclude the agencies'.

§ `Section 4.1: comity`

§§ `4.1 The factors the agencies weigh`

-- 4.1 [pdf pages 30-32], in the text's order: the first paragraph's "whether significant
-- interests of any foreign sovereign would be affected"; the five factors of the list "Among
-- other things, the Agencies weigh"; and the "Second" discussion's encouragement or
-- discouragement by the foreign sovereign. The "Third" discussion (whether foreign
-- enforcement could achieve the objectives) is the fifth factor's own gloss.
DECLARE `Comity consideration (4.1)` IS ONE OF
    `whether significant interests of any foreign sovereign would be affected`
    `the existence of a purpose to affect or an actual effect on U.S. commerce`
    `the significance and foreseeability of the effects of the anticompetitive conduct on the United States`
    `the degree of conflict with a foreign jurisdiction's law or articulated policy`
    `the extent to which the enforcement activities of another jurisdiction, including remedies resulting from those enforcement activities, may be affected`
    `the effectiveness of foreign enforcement as compared to U.S. enforcement`
    `the extent to which a foreign sovereign encourages or discourages certain courses of conduct or leaves parties free to choose among different courses of conduct`

-- A report of the considerations the caller says are present, in the text's order. No
-- weighing, no bottom line: the text gives none, and says a decision to proceed "represents
-- a determination that the importance of antitrust enforcement outweighs any relevant
-- foreign policy concerns" [pdf page 31], which is the agencies' own determination.
@ref 4.1 [pdf pages 30-32]; n.103, n.104 [pdf page 30]
GIVEN c IS A `Comity facts (4.1)`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Comity consideration (4.1)`
`4.1: the comity considerations that are present` MEANS
    catMaybes (LIST (IF c's `significant interests of a foreign sovereign would be affected` THEN JUST `whether significant interests of any foreign sovereign would be affected` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF c's `there is a purpose to affect, or an actual effect on, U.S. commerce` THEN JUST `the existence of a purpose to affect or an actual effect on U.S. commerce` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF c's `the effects of the conduct on the United States are significant and foreseeable` THEN JUST `the significance and foreseeability of the effects of the anticompetitive conduct on the United States` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF c's `enforcement would conflict with a foreign jurisdiction's law or articulated policy` THEN JUST `the degree of conflict with a foreign jurisdiction's law or articulated policy` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF c's `the enforcement activities of another jurisdiction, including their remedies, may be affected` THEN JUST `the extent to which the enforcement activities of another jurisdiction, including remedies resulting from those enforcement activities, may be affected` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF c's `the objectives sought by U.S. enforcement could be achieved by foreign enforcement` THEN JUST `the effectiveness of foreign enforcement as compared to U.S. enforcement` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF c's `a foreign sovereign encourages or discourages the course of conduct, or leaves parties free to choose among courses of conduct` THEN JUST `the extent to which a foreign sovereign encourages or discourages certain courses of conduct or leaves parties free to choose among different courses of conduct` ELSE NOTHING))

§§ `4.1 Conflict of law`

DECLARE `What section 4.1 says of a conflict of law` IS ONE OF
    `no conflict of law exists, because a person subject to the laws of both sovereigns can comply with both`
    `no conflict exists, because the foreign law is neutral as to the conduct`
    `the agencies do not view the mere existence of a blocking statute as creating a conflict of law for purposes of the comity analysis`
    `a conflict of law exists, and comity may counsel in favor of declining enforcement`

-- 4.1 [pdf pages 31-32]: "no conflict of law exists if a person subject to the laws of two
-- sovereigns can comply with both. Moreover, no conflict exists in cases where foreign law is
-- neutral as to particular conduct, because it remains possible for the parties in question
-- to comply with the U.S. antitrust laws without violating foreign law. In situations where a
-- conflict of law exists, however, comity may counsel in favor of declining enforcement."
-- n.144 [pdf page 42]: "The Agencies do not view the mere existence of blocking statutes as
-- creating a conflict of law for purposes of the comity analysis." The neutral-law sentence
-- is unconditional and wins over a caller who says compliance with both is impossible (fork
-- F7). A person who cannot comply with both, under a foreign law that is not neutral and is
-- more than a blocking statute's existence, is read as a conflict of law (fork F7).
@ref 4.1 [pdf pages 31-32]; n.108 [pdf page 32]; n.144 [pdf page 42]
GIVEN l IS A `Foreign law and the conduct (4.1)`
GIVETH A `What section 4.1 says of a conflict of law`
`4.1: whether a conflict of law exists` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF l's `the foreign law is neutral as to the conduct`
      THEN `no conflict exists, because the foreign law is neutral as to the conduct`
      IF l's `a person subject to the laws of both sovereigns can comply with both`
      THEN `no conflict of law exists, because a person subject to the laws of both sovereigns can comply with both`
      IF l's `the only foreign law said to conflict is a blocking statute, shown by nothing more than its existence`
      THEN `the agencies do not view the mere existence of a blocking statute as creating a conflict of law for purposes of the comity analysis`
      OTHERWISE `a conflict of law exists, and comity may counsel in favor of declining enforcement`

§§ `4.1 A foreign authority's investigation or action`

DECLARE `What section 4.1 says of a foreign authority's action` IS ONE OF
    `it will not preclude an investigation or enforcement action by either the Department or the Commission: the Agency will determine whether, in light of the foreign authority's actions, investigation or enforcement is warranted, and may coordinate with that authority`

-- 4.1 [pdf page 31]: "An investigation or enforcement action by a foreign authority will not
-- preclude an investigation or enforcement action by either the Department or the
-- Commission. Rather, the Agency will determine whether, in light of actions by the foreign
-- authority, investigation or enforcement is warranted ... it may coordinate with that
-- authority." Without a foreign investigation or action the sentence states nothing.
@ref 4.1 [pdf page 31]; n.107 [pdf page 31]
GIVEN foreign IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 4.1 says of a foreign authority's action`
`4.1: what a foreign authority's investigation or action does, if there is one` foreign MEANS
    IF foreign
    THEN JUST `it will not preclude an investigation or enforcement action by either the Department or the Commission: the Agency will determine whether, in light of the foreign authority's actions, investigation or enforcement is warranted, and may coordinate with that authority`
    ELSE NOTHING
